            <div xml:id="d259">

                <pb facs="0397" n="285" sameAs="frus:frus1910/pg285"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 5315/981.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Reid to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-06-08"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">June 8, 1910</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1310.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my cable of the 24th
                    ultimo relative to the signing of the Hukuang loan by the representatives of the
                    groups of the four powers in Paris, I have the honor to inclose herewith copy of
                    a note from the foreign office dated June 7, which recites the terms of the
                    agreement in question.</p>
                <p>The British Government approves the arrangement come to and, subject to the
                    concurrence of the three other Governments concerned, consider that the Chinese
                    Government should now be formally requested without delay to complete the loan
                    agreement of June 6, 1909, already initialed by them, as well as the subsequent
                    supplementary agreements entered into with a view to the inclusion of the
                    American group.</p>
                <p>Sir Edward Grey suggests that the representatives of the four powers at Peking
                    should be instructed to address an identic note to the Wai-wu Pu on the lines
                    indicated, and expresses himself as of the opinion that no time should be lost
                    in sending out the instructions to tne representatives at Peking.</p>
                <p>The substance of the note in question was cabled to the department to-day.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Whitelaw Reid.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d259-a1.xml" xpointer="d259-a1"/>
            </div>
